== G.1 How a Written Answer Is Marked

#table(
  columns: (1.1fr, 2.5fr, 1.0fr),
  inset: (x: 6pt, y: 3.4pt),
  align: (left + horizon, left + horizon, center + horizon),
  stroke: 0.4pt + jamb-border,
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { jamb-navy } else if calc.odd(y) { white } else { jamb-light },
  table.header(
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Move],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[What it looks like],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Marks],
  ),
  [Define], [one sentence with the class and the difference], [1],
  [Name], [the correct term, spelled correctly], [1],
  [State], [a short complete sentence — not a phrase], [1 each],
  [Explain], [a "because" clause that names the process], [2],
  [Give examples], [a named crop, tool or place, not "some crops"], [1 each],
  [Compare], [the criterion first, then both sides of it], [2],
)

#v(4pt)

#text(size: 9.4pt)[
  In a "state four" question, four short sentences with a number each is the safest form. In an
  "explain" question the mark is in the causal word; a list of facts without "because" collects
  half. Never answer a "state" question with a paragraph — the examiner stops reading at the
  first fully correct point and divides what is left among the rest.
]

== G.2 Answer One — The Importance of Agriculture

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[State five ways in which agriculture contributes to the Nigerian economy.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *1. Food supply.* Agriculture feeds the population: cassava, yam, rice, maize, sorghum and
  millet supply the bulk of the calories, and the livestock and fisheries sub-sectors supply
  the protein.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *2. Employment.* Farming, processing and marketing employ more Nigerians than any other single
  sector, and most of that employment is rural.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *3. Foreign exchange.* Cocoa, sesame, cashew, ginger and hides earn dollars; before oil, the
  groundnut and cocoa exports paid for the country's imports.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *4. Raw materials.* Cotton feeds the textile mill, groundnut and palm produce feed the oil
  mill, rubber feeds the tyre factory, and sugar cane feeds the refinery.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *5. Capital and revenue.* Agriculture generates savings that are invested elsewhere, and the
  taxes and levies on farm produce contribute to government revenue.
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 9.2pt)[
  *Two more that earn a mark when asked for six:* it earns foreign exchange indirectly by
  reducing food imports, and it supports the rural infrastructure — roads, markets and
  storage — on which other rural activity depends.
]

== G.3 Answer Two — Why a Legume in the Rotation

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[Explain why a legume is included in a crop rotation.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  A legume such as cowpea, groundnut or soya bean carries *Rhizobium* bacteria in nodules on its
  roots, and those bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into a form the plant can use. When the crop
  is harvested and the roots and stover are left in the soil, the nitrogen enters the soil
  organic matter and becomes available to the following cereal. The rotation therefore *adds*
  nitrogen where a cereal would have *removed* it, and the farmer can reduce the nitrogen in the
  next top dressing.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  The second gain is the break in the pest cycle. A legume is attacked by different insects and
  different root diseases from a cereal, so a root-feeding nematode that has built up under two
  seasons of maize finds no host in the legume year and its population falls. A third gain is
  the improvement in structure: the legume's taproot and its dense root mass open the soil, and
  its residues add organic matter.
]

== G.4 Answer Three — Soil Erosion on a Slope

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[A farmer's field on a 10° slope is losing soil. State four measures and explain how each works.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *1. Contour ploughing.* The furrows run across the slope instead of up and down it, so each
  furrow checks the water and gives it time to soak in rather than run off.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *2. Terracing.* The slope is cut into level steps, which reduces the gradient the water travels
  on and shortens the distance before it is intercepted.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *3. Strip cropping or a cover crop.* Alternate strips of a dense crop and a row crop, or a
  creeping cover such as melon, protect the surface from raindrop impact and slow the flow
  between the strips.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *4. Mulching.* A layer of crop residue absorbs the energy of the raindrops, keeps the surface
  open and reduces both the splash and the speed of the runoff.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *5. Planting a grass strip or a hedge along the contour.* The roots bind the soil and the stems
  filter the sediment out of the water before it reaches the bottom of the field.
]

