== G.6 Answer Five — A Seed That Will Not Germinate

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[A farmer's maize seed fails to germinate in the field though the seed was bought fresh. Give four possible causes.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *1. The seed was sown too deep.* Maize needs about 4–5 cm; below that the shoot exhausts its
  food store before it reaches the surface.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *2. The soil was too dry or too wet.* Dry soil leaves the seed unable to absorb water; a
  waterlogged soil starves the embryo of oxygen.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *3. The seed was damaged in storage.* Heat, damp or a storage insecticide applied too heavily
  can kill the embryo while the seed still looks sound; a germination test would reveal it.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *4. The seed was planted into a crusted or compacted surface.* A hard cap that the coleoptile
  cannot push through traps the germinating seed below the surface.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *5. Soil pests or a seed-borne fungus.* Termites, wireworms and damping-off fungi attack the
  seed and the young shoot, and the gap in the stand is the evidence.
]

== G.7 Answer Six — The Case for Asexual Propagation

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[State four advantages and two disadvantages of asexual propagation.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Advantages.* First, the offspring is genetically identical to the parent, so a superior
  variety — a high-yielding cassava, a resistant cocoa clone — is reproduced exactly. Second, the
  new plant is mature tissue, so it comes into bearing sooner: a budded citrus fruits in three
  years where a seedling takes six or seven. Third, a plant that sets little or no viable seed,
  such as the seedless banana or the improved pineapple, can still be multiplied. Fourth, a
  rootstock can be chosen for a soil or a disease, as when citrus is budded onto a tristeza-
  tolerant rootstock.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Disadvantages.* The planting material is bulky and perishable — cassava stems and yam setts
  must be moved and planted within days — and the method is labour-intensive. More seriously,
  because every plant is a clone, a disease or a pest that defeats one defeats them all, and any
  virus present in the parent is carried into every offspring.
]

== G.8 Answer Seven — The Maize Stem Borer

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[Describe the damage caused by the maize stem borer and state three control measures.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  The larva bores into the funnel of the young plant and then into the stem, leaving a row of
  small holes and a sawdust-like frass at the entrance. The growing point is destroyed, so the
  centre leaf wilts and dies — "dead heart" — and the stem weakens and snaps in wind. Later
  generations tunnel in the cob stalk and reduce the grain.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Control.* Destroy the crop residue after harvest, since the larvae overwinter in the stubble.
  Plant early and at the recommended spacing so that the stand passes the vulnerable stage
  together. Scout the funnel at two and four weeks and apply a granular insecticide into the
  funnel only if the damage reaches the threshold; and rotate maize with a non-host such as
  cowpea.
]

== G.9 Answer Eight — Why Store Produce Dry

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[Explain why grain must be dried before storage and state the danger of storing it wet.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  Grain is dried to about 12–13 per cent moisture because above that figure two things happen at
  once. First, the moulds that cause heating and aflatoxin grow freely, and a damp bulk of maize
  can heat to the point of spontaneous combustion. Second, the storage insects — weevils and
  grain borers — breed faster in damp grain, and their feeding raises the moisture further.
  Drying also reduces the mass that has to be carried and prevents caking in the bag.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  The danger of storing wet grain is therefore a double loss: the grain is spoiled by mould
  before the insect arrives, and the aflatoxin that the mould produces is a poison that survives
  cooking and cannot be washed out. A bag of damp maize is not only a lost bag; it is a health
  risk to the household that eats it.
]

== G.10 Answer Nine — Selling at Harvest or Later

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[A farmer must decide whether to sell maize at harvest or to store it and sell three months later. Advise her.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  The decision is arithmetic, not sentiment. Let the harvest price be ₦300 per kg and the
  three-month price ₦420; the gain is ₦120 per kg, or 40 per cent. Against that gain set the
  costs: the store, the bags, the drying, the insecticide and the loss in weight and quality. If
  a 5 per cent weight loss and a 3 per cent mould loss are expected, the effective gain falls to
  about ₦90 per kg. Then add the risk: fire, rodents, theft and a price fall.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  The rule is to store only what the farmer can dry, protect and finance. A farmer without a
  clean, sealed store and without working capital should sell at harvest and avoid the loss.
  A farmer with a store, a moisture meter and a cooperative that can advance credit should store,
  and should sell in the scarcity months of May to July, not in the first week of the rise.
]

== G.11 Answer Ten — A Crop Rotation for Four Years

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[Design a four-year rotation for a maize–cowpea–cassava farm and justify the order.]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Year 1: maize.* A heavy feeder that exploits the fertility built up in the previous legume
  year, and its deep roots use the topsoil before the cassava takes over.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Year 2: cowpea.* The legume fixes nitrogen and breaks the stem-borer cycle that the maize
  year encouraged; its residue is light and does not shade the following crop.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Year 3: cassava.* A long-season, low-input crop that draws on the residual fertility and
  tolerates a poorer soil; it also shades out the weeds that the two short crops left behind.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  *Year 4: fallow, or a green manure such as mucuna.* The land rests, the organic matter
  recovers and the nematode population falls before maize returns.
]

#text(size: 9.4pt)[
  The order obeys three rules: heavy feeder after legume, shallow root after deep, and a
  different plant family each year. It also spreads labour — cassava's peak is at planting,
  maize's at harvest — across the season.
]

