== G.13 Answer Twelve — Farm Records and the Loan

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 8pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.7pt)[Why does a bank ask a farmer for records before lending?]
]

#v(3pt)

#text(size: 10pt)[
  Records are the only evidence a lender has that the farm is a business and not a hope. A
  well-kept set shows the size of the previous harvest, the cost of producing it and the price at
  which it was sold, so the bank can estimate the cash the farm will have when the loan falls
  due. It shows whether the farm has repaid a loan before. It shows the assets that can stand as
  security and the stock that can be counted.
]

#text(size: 10pt)[
  Without records the lender must rely on the farmer's word, and the risk premium rises until the
  loan is either refused or priced beyond the farm's margin. This is why the cooperative that
  keeps group records can borrow where its individual members cannot: the record replaces the
  reputation the individual has not yet built.
]

